---
import CaseStudy from "@/layouts/CaseStudy.astro";
import { projects } from "@/data/projects";

const project = projects.find((p) => p.slug === "alloy-mentors")!;
---

<CaseStudy project={project}>
  <div class="prose-case" data-reveal>
    <h2>Where it started</h2>
    <p>
      Alloy began as a volunteer hour-tracker for a single program. Hours turned out to be the least interesting thing
      a mentoring program measures. What coordinators actually needed to show — to funders, to schools, to parents —
      was whether the students were growing, and nothing they used recorded that.
    </p>

    <h2>What it became</h2>
    <p>
      So the product was rebuilt around the relationship between a mentor and a student. Every student gets goals with
      checkpoints, a skills grid, and a timeline that fills in as sessions are logged. At the end of a term, that
      record becomes a report a program can hand a funder, or a short plain-language note for a guardian — produced
      from work that already happened, not assembled by hand in May.
    </p>
    <p>
      Then it stopped being one program's tool. Any organization can now create itself inside the app, choose which
      features it needs, and use its own words for its people. A tutoring business, a coaching club, and a school
      volunteer program each see an app that looks like it was built for them, because in every way that matters,
      it was.
    </p>

    <h2>Built for minors</h2>
    <p>
      Most participants in these programs are under eighteen, and that shaped the architecture more than any feature
      did. Tenancy is enforced in the database rather than in the interface, student accounts see only their own
      record, and check-in codes are signed on the device and expire within about a minute. Security work here isn't a phase at
      the end — the access policies have been through an adversarial review, written up and fixed like any other bug.
    </p>
  </div>
</CaseStudy>
